<Project>
    <PropertyGroup>
        <!-- Version - Change this ONE place to update all projects -->
        <Version>1.9.2</Version>
        <AssemblyVersion>1.9.2.0</AssemblyVersion>
        <FileVersion>1.9.2.0</FileVersion>

        <!-- Package Metadata -->
        <Authors>Jesse Carlberg</Authors>
        <Company>Jesse Carlberg Production LLC</Company>
        <Product>MillWorks AuditCore</Product>
        <Description>Tamper-evident audit logging and compliance platform for .NET</Description>
        <Copyright>Copyright © 2025-2026 MillWorks</Copyright>

        <!-- Package Settings -->
        <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/jesserules/millworks.auditcore</PackageProjectUrl>
        <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/jesserules/millworks.auditcore</RepositoryUrl>
        <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
        <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
        <PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>false</PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>
        <PackageTags>audit;logging;compliance;security;tamper-detection;efcore;aspnetcore</PackageTags>
        <PackageReleaseNotes>v1.9.2: completes the security-event append-only cleanup. Removes the Status property (and IX_SecurityEvents_Status index) from AuditSecurityEventEntity, Status from SecurityEventDto, the GetOpenEventsAsync method (from IAuditSecurityEventService, AuditSecurityEventService, ISecurityEventRepository, SecurityEventRepository), and the SecurityEventStatus enum. A schema-only migration (SecurityEventStatusRemoval) drops the Status column and index from the SecurityEvents table. Security events are immutable facts with no in-AuditCore lifecycle; triage/resolution is owned by the application security layer (MillWorks.Security). BREAKING, but no AuditCore or MillWorks consumer referenced these members (verified 2026-06-28). See CHANGELOG.md.

Earlier release notes follow below:

v1.9.1: removes the now-inert security-event resolution columns. `ResolvedAt`, `ResolvedBy`, and `Resolution` are dropped from AuditSecurityEventEntity, SecurityEventDto, and the entity/DTO mappings, with a schema-only migration (SecurityEventAppendOnlyCleanup) dropping those columns from the SecurityEvents table. These became dead in 1.9.0 when ResolveEventAsync was removed (security events are append-only; triage/resolution is owned by the application security layer). Status and GetOpenEventsAsync are unchanged. Removing public SecurityEventDto properties is technically breaking, but no AuditCore or MillWorks consumer reads these fields (verified 2026-06-28). See CHANGELOG.md.

Earlier release notes follow below:

v1.9.0: adds append-only immutability enforcement and makes security events append-only. New dependency-free IAppendOnlyEntity marker in MillWorks.AuditCore.Abstractions and a sealed AppendOnlyInterceptor (EF Core SaveChanges interceptor) in MillWorks.AuditCore.EntityFramework: a marked entity entering Modified or Deleted state throws InvalidOperationException on save. The AppendOnlyEntity base class implements the marker, so AuditLogs and AuditIntegrity are enforced insert-only, and the interceptor is wired onto AuditDbContext automatically by UseEntityFramework. ExecuteUpdate/ExecuteDelete and raw SQL bypass the change tracker and the guard by design (sanctioned retention pruning / GDPR/HIPAA erasure). Consumer entities can adopt the marker directly without an EF Core dependency. AuditSecurityEventEntity now implements IAppendOnlyEntity — recorded security events are immutable. BREAKING: IAuditSecurityEventService.ResolveEventAsync was removed; security-event triage and resolution lifecycle are owned by the application security layer (MillWorks.Security), not AuditCore. See CHANGELOG.md for full details.

Earlier release notes follow below:

v1.6.2: patch over v1.6.1 closing the retry half of the strict-mode-tamper chained bug. Even with v1.6.1's nested-transaction guard, any failure inside AuditLogger's atomic event+integrity transaction (applock timeout, digital-signature error, transient DB blip) rolls back but leaves the added AuditEventEntity tracked on the scoped AuditApplicationDbContext. The pre-fix ResilientAuditLogger retry path invoked the same innerLogger against the same scoped context, so attempt 2's AddAsync threw "The instance of entity type 'AuditEventEntity' cannot be tracked because another instance with the same key value for {'EventId'} is already being tracked" — masking the original failure and dead-lettering every event. v1.6.2 makes ResilientAuditLogger.LogAsync and LogBatchAsync create a fresh DI scope per retry attempt and resolve a fresh AuditLogger (with a fresh AuditApplicationDbContext) from it. The decorated innerLogger is retained for BeginOperationAsync / EndOperationAsync / CreateScope, which depend on a single AuditLogger instance's _activeOperations dictionary. AuditLogger is unsealed with LogAsync/LogBatchAsync virtual to let tests subclass. AuditLogger is now registered as its concrete scoped type with IAuditLogger forwarding to the same instance so the per-retry scope can resolve AuditLogger concretely without recursing through the decorator. BREAKING: ResilientAuditLogger constructor now takes an IServiceScopeFactory (before the logger/diagnostics params). Consumers constructing ResilientAuditLogger manually outside UseResilience must pass one. New regression ResilientAuditLoggerRetrySqliteTests asserts retry succeeds, the event commits exactly once, and the DLQ stays empty; verified red on pre-fix and green with the fix. Upgrade from v1.6.1 recommended for any consumer running strict-mode tamper detection under ResilientAuditLogger.

v1.6.1 content follows below:

v1.6.1: critical patch over v1.6.0. TamperDetectionService.CreateIntegrityRecord[Batch]Async now joins an outer transaction already owned by the shared DbContext instead of nesting its own. When AuditLogger wraps event+integrity in a single atomic transaction (strict mode, EnableTamperDetection=true, EnableBatchedIntegrityWrites=false), v1.6.0's unconditional ExecuteInTransactionAsync opened a nested transaction that EF rejected with "connection already in a transaction"; the rollback stranded the added AuditEventEntity in the change tracker and ResilientAuditLogger's retry hit an identity-map conflict on the same EventId. v1.6.1 checks IAuditIntegrityRepository.CurrentTransaction and, if an outer transaction is active, joins it and binds sp_getapplock to it; otherwise opens its own. New regression test AuditLoggerTamperNestedTransactionTests covers the exact path. Upgrade from v1.6.0 recommended for any consumer running strict-mode tamper detection.

v1.6.0 content follows below:

v1.6.0: fixes the remaining cross-process duplicate-key race on AuditIntegrity.SequenceNumber and lands the queued ProdHardening phase 3–6 work. (1) TamperDetectionService now serializes hash-chain appends with SQL Server sp_getapplock (@LockOwner='Transaction', resource 'audit:integrity:append') inside the integrity-write transaction. The prior IAuditDistributedLockService path was either process-local (in-memory default — ineffective across API replicas) or Redis-leased with a 5s TTL that could expire mid-critical-section; both allowed concurrent writers to collide on IX_AuditIntegrity_SequenceNumber. The applock is bound to the transaction, auto-releases on commit/rollback/connection drop, and has no TTL to expire. A 32-parallel-writer SQL Server regression test proves the fix: green with applock, 49 retry-log entries without. (2) Non-SQL-Server providers (SQLite test harness) are serialized by a process-local semaphore gated on IAuditIntegrityRepository.SupportsCrossProcessAppendLock. (3) The retry loop remains as defense-in-depth; after this change, a non-zero retry count in production is a signal that the applock was removed or weakened. Also includes ProdHardening phases 3–6: runtime EF schema selection via HasDefaultSchema, fail-closed audit policy (AuditFailureMode + IAuditFailurePolicy + RegulatedEntityFailurePolicy), request-audit overflow policy with DLQ routing and shutdown drain, and a dedicated SQL Server Testcontainers lane. BREAKING: TamperDetectionService constructor no longer takes IAuditDistributedLockService; the lock service interface stays registered for DeadLetterQueueProcessor and other coordination primitives. IAuditIntegrityRepository gained SupportsCrossProcessAppendLock and AcquireAppendLockAsync.</PackageReleaseNotes>

        <!-- Symbols & Source Link -->
        <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
        <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>

        <!-- Build Settings -->
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
        <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
        <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
        <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
        <TreatWarningsAsErrors>false</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

        <!-- Ensure project references become package dependencies when packing -->
        <IncludeReferencedProjects>true</IncludeReferencedProjects>

        <!-- Deterministic builds for CI -->
        <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.SourceLink.GitHub" Version="8.0.0" PrivateAssets="All" />
    </ItemGroup>
</Project>
